\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Coordinate symmetries of momentum blocks}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{On a periodic grid, shifting a Fourier mode by one site multiplies it
by a scalar. Each coordinate direction has its own multiplier. Reversing
a direction can change its multiplier, and exchanging two directions
can move a field between the momentum blocks of \ref{fdip-000S}.
Which blocks can be reached from one another? Which coordinate symmetries
still act within a single block?}

\p{When every period is at least three, translations and
period-preserving signed permutations form the
normalizer of translations (\ref{fdip-0166}) in the nearest-neighbor
graph. The subgroup fixing a label factors over equal periods and
pairs of opposite residues (\ref{fdip-0168}). Over a field with the
required roots and invertible grid order, the representation on an
orbit is induced from the stabilizer action on one block
(\ref{fdip-016E}). An irreducible representation on the orbit
corresponds to an irreducible action on that block (\ref{fdip-016F}).}

\p{On a graded spin space each signed change of coordinates has
two lifts differing by the central sign (\ref{fdip-016U}). The center
of the lifted group can contain further elements (\ref{fdip-016V}).
A lift preserves the two sign spaces of the chosen grading when its
coordinate change preserves orientation, and exchanges them when it
reverses orientation (\ref{fdip-016Z}).}

\transclude{fdip-0162}
\transclude{fdip-016A}
\transclude{fdip-016N}
